2186407919 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407919 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407919 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407919 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186407901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407919 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ef9310b5-68fe-5622-806f-a4683ba15fec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407920 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407920 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81292" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407920 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407920 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/df/df3acf049579ec4de948d84d04d4fe3012c2232b9699d0471857227f52c78107.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $x={y}^{2},\\,$ $x=8-{y}^{2},$ and $y=0,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dA=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407920 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "60195b0c-70fb-5843-93ba-a36d5b0f7537" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407920 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-{y}^{2} & ={y}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =2{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/27/2722224678aeb1f9ea64f42de2dc4fbf6e139350c291de16500957575fd23850.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le 8-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{8-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{8-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407922 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407922 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81390" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407922 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407922 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1b/1b03f2ecd03a89d9484e7d28ab4cb34656b288c3a4554c9a4eeacbe9b61f692c.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}x\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and $y=2-x,$ above the line $y=1,$ as shown in the picture." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407922 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af73e85d-c908-5a06-97f4-04484193b6c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407922 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $D$ is a type II region. Since $x\\ge 0,$ we obtain the following equations in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} \\\\ y=2-x & \\,⟹\\,x=2-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} & =2-y \\\\ 4-{y}^{2} & =(2-y)^{2} \\\\ 4-{y}^{2} & =4-4y+{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-4y & =0 \\\\ y(y-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the line $y=1,$ the $y$-limits are \n$1\\le y\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/f8/f8fedf15e26f64fc344bd7db6ffaa659bd7c130f36f4a39389b6108136836104.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,2-y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\,dA={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{2-y}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,x\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{2-y}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,x\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{2-y}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}x\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\frac{1}{2}[\\,{x}^{2}\\,]_{2-y}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\text{ }dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\frac{1}{2}(4-{y}^{2}-(2-y)^{2})dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,(2y-{y}^{2})dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,(2y-{y}^{2})dy & ={[{y}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(4-\\frac{8}{3})-(1-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407923 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186407927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407923 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "04ef475a-c5cf-51ff-ad2a-7ea0479c4f76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407923 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186323930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407924 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186407925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407924 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1ac49a49-1255-59a8-8c4e-cccd1b1d9a34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407924 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186407920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407925 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6b60b51b-f512-550b-bd86-63bfa17217f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407925 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186407922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407926 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186407924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407926 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ac90c75a-a52d-53a2-be62-82bea3febc78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407926 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186134519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407927 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186407926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407927 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e5e63a66-bea9-51c1-84d4-5364df4be361" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407927 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186407921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428679 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428679 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account fou